WB.L("sydney-opera-house-story", "arts", "beginner", "The Sydney Opera House: a building that almost wasn't",
  "It is the most recognisable building in Australia, yet it cost about fourteen times its first estimate, lost its architect halfway through and took fourteen years to build. Here is the remarkable story behind the sails.",
  [
    ["A sketch that won", "An almost unknown 38-year-old Danish architect won the international competition in 1957 with loose sketches of white shells.",
      "In 1955 the New South Wales government launched an international competition to design an opera house on Bennelong Point, a rocky spit in Sydney Harbour that had held a tram depot. More than two hundred entries arrived. The winner, announced in January 1957, was Jørn Utzon, a 38-year-old Danish architect who had built very little. His entry was hardly more than loose, beautiful sketches of white curved shells rising from a massive stone platform.\n\nA popular story says judge Eero Saarinen rescued it from a pile of rejects. True or not, the jury saw something nobody else had imagined: a building meant to be seen from every angle, including above."],
    ["More than a place name", "Bennelong Point is named for Woollarawarre Bennelong, and the harbour foreshore was a gathering place long before colonisation.",
      "The site carries the name of Woollarawarre Bennelong, a Wangal man of the Eora nation, captured in 1789 on the orders of Governor Arthur Phillip, who hoped to learn about the local people through him. Bennelong learned English, became a go-between for the colonists and the Eora, and later travelled to England, one of the first Aboriginal people known to do so. A small brick hut was built for him on the point.\n\nLong before this, the foreshore was a meeting and gathering place for its traditional custodians. Today the Opera House acknowledges that history, often hosting First Nations performances and projections on its sails, linking one of the world's newest landmarks to one of its oldest living cultures."],
    ["Solving the shells", "Cutting every shell from the surface of one imaginary sphere made the sails buildable from repeating precast pieces.",
      "Nobody knew how to build Utzon's sketch. The free-flowing curves had no fixed geometry, so every piece would be unique and the shells might not stand. Construction began in 1959 under political pressure, with the design unresolved, a decision that caused enormous trouble later.\n\nUtzon and engineers Ove Arup and Partners spent years testing shapes, using some of the earliest computers on a building project. The breakthrough, around 1961 to 1962, is usually credited to Utzon: every shell would be cut from the surface of the same imaginary sphere. The ribs could then be made from repeating precast concrete segments, built on site and lifted by crane.\n\nThe sails are covered in over a million Swedish ceramic tiles, glossy white and matte cream, so the roof seems to change colour through the day."],
    ["The architect walks away", "Soaring costs and a dispute with a new minister led Utzon to resign in 1966, and he never returned to see the finished building.",
      "To pay for the project, the state ran the Opera House Lottery, which raised much of the money. Thousands of Australians bought tickets and helped build a landmark.\n\nBy the mid 1960s the project was years late and costs were soaring. After a change of government in 1965, the new Minister for Public Works, Davis Hughes, clashed with Utzon over money, design changes and control, and payments to the architect were held back. Utzon resigned in February 1966. Street protests called for his return, but he left Australia with his family and never came back.\n\nAustralian architects led by Peter Hall finished the interiors with major changes. The larger hall, meant for opera, became a concert hall, hence complaints about the cramped opera stage and pit."],
    ["The bill and the reconciliation", "The Opera House opened in 1973 at about fourteen times its first estimate, and Utzon was honoured before his death in 2008.",
      "Queen Elizabeth II opened the Opera House on 20 October 1973, fourteen years after construction began. The first estimate was around seven million dollars. The final bill was about one hundred and two million, often quoted as a warning about big public projects. Yet critics soon fell quiet. It became the symbol of Sydney and Australia, hosting over a thousand performances a year.\n\nIn the late 1990s the trust asked Utzon, then in his eighties, to set out design principles for the building's future. He worked with his son Jan and architect Richard Johnson, though he never visited Sydney again. The Utzon Room was redesigned to his plans. He received the Pritzker Prize in 2003, and in 2007 the Opera House joined the UNESCO World Heritage List. Utzon died in 2008 without seeing it in person."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was unusual about the design that won the 1957 competition?", ["It was mostly loose sketches by a little-known architect","It was a finished set of detailed engineering plans","It copied a famous design from an earlier competition","It was designed by a team of local Sydney engineers"], 0, "The jury backed a bold vision, even though the sketches did not say how it could be built."],
    [1, "What does the name Bennelong Point honour?", ["The Danish architect who designed the building","Woollarawarre Bennelong, a Wangal man of the Eora","The first governor of the colony, Arthur Phillip","A harbour pilot who first charted the point"], 1, "The point is named for Woollarawarre Bennelong, linking the building to a much older story."],
    [2, "How did cutting every shell from one imaginary sphere help the builders?", ["It allowed the shells to be built from timber instead of concrete","It made every tile on the roof a different shape","Matching curves let ribs be made from repeating precast segments","It removed the need for engineers to use computers"], 2, "A shared geometry turned a design with unique pieces into one that could be mass produced on site."],
    [3, "Why did Utzon leave the project in 1966?", ["Engineers found the sails were structurally unsafe","He accepted a bigger commission back in Denmark","The lottery ran out of money to pay his fees","A dispute with the new minister over costs and control"], 3, "Rising costs and clashing control led to his resignation, and the interiors changed greatly after he left."],
    [4, "What happened to the Opera House's reputation after it opened?", ["It became a beloved national symbol despite the cost","It was demolished soon after because the final cost was too high","It stayed unpopular with the public because of the cost blowout","It lost its listing after the architect resigned from the project"], 0, "Critics quieted as the building became the symbol of Sydney, and it was later World Heritage listed."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"It cost fourteen times its estimate and lost its architect. Here is the story."});

WB.L("gothic-cathedrals", "arts", "intermediate", "Gothic cathedrals: how medieval builders made stone fly",
  "Without calculators or engineering degrees, medieval masons raised stone ceilings dozens of metres into the air and filled walls with glowing glass. Here is how they pulled it off.",
  [
    ["An abbot who wanted light", "Gothic architecture began in the 1140s when Abbot Suger rebuilt Saint-Denis to glow with light.",
      "In the 1140s Suger, abbot of Saint-Denis just north of Paris, set out to rebuild the choir of his abbey church, the burial place of French kings. Influenced by the idea that God could be experienced as light, he wanted a church that glowed. His builders combined existing techniques, the pointed arch, ribbed vaults and large windows, in a new way. The result, consecrated in 1144, felt astonishingly open and bright compared with the thick-walled, shadowy Romanesque churches before it. Historians treat it as the birth of Gothic.\n\nThe style spread through travelling master masons. The name 'Gothic' came later, as a Renaissance insult blaming the style on the Goths."],
    ["Arches and flying buttresses", "Pointed arches and flying buttresses carried the vault's weight outside the walls, freeing them for huge windows.",
      "A pointed arch pushes outwards less than a semicircular arch of the same width. It is also adaptable: a round arch's height is fixed by its width, but a pointed one can be steeper or flatter, so narrow and wide arches reach the same height. With ribbed vaults, a skeleton of stone ribs carrying the ceiling, masons could roof complicated spaces.\n\nA stone vault constantly pushes the tops of the walls outwards. Romanesque builders used thick walls and small windows. Gothic builders moved the support outside. A flying buttress is a half arch that leaps across the side aisle to a stone pier, catching the vault's thrust and channelling it to the ground. Heavy pinnacles on the piers help. The walls no longer held much up, so they became enormous windows."],
    ["Walls of coloured light", "Stained glass, made with metal oxides and lead, turned cathedral walls into glowing picture books.",
      "Step inside Chartres Cathedral on a sunny day and the walls seem to dissolve into deep blues and reds. Medieval stained glass was made by adding metal oxides to molten glass: cobalt for blue, copper for red and green. Glaziers cut the coloured pieces, painted details such as faces and folds in dark enamel fired onto the surface, and joined everything with lead strips. Iron frames held the panels.\n\nThe windows told Bible stories and saints' lives, and often showed the trades who paid for them, such as bakers or shoemakers. Circular rose windows, divided like flower petals, became a signature of the style. The deep blue of Chartres has been admired for centuries and is still hard to reproduce exactly."],
    ["Building without equations", "Master masons relied on geometry, experience and rules of thumb, learning from failures such as Beauvais.",
      "Medieval builders had no theory of forces in the modern sense. Master masons worked with geometry, using compasses, set squares and string to lay out proportions, and guarded rules of thumb passed down through apprenticeships. Designs were drawn full size on plaster floors called tracing floors, from which templates for each stone were cut.\n\nBuilders learned by trial and, sometimes, disaster. At Beauvais in northern France, where they attempted the tallest Gothic choir of all, with a vault about forty-eight metres high, part of the vault collapsed in 1284 and was rebuilt with extra supports. The work was also slow. Cologne Cathedral was begun in 1248 and not finished until 1880, after centuries of interruption."],
    ["Gothic comes to Australia", "The nineteenth century Gothic Revival spread across the British world, leaving cathedrals and universities in Australia.",
      "In the 1800s Gothic made a spectacular comeback. Architects in Britain saw it as a deeply Christian and national style, and the rebuilt Houses of Parliament in London became its most famous example. Writers found Gothic buildings romantic and mysterious, and the word lent its name to spooky novels set in crumbling castles. It is now a neutral description.\n\nThe Revival travelled with the British Empire, and Australia is full of it. William Wardell designed both St Mary's Cathedral in Sydney and St Patrick's Cathedral in Melbourne, with pointed arches, rib vaults and stained glass in homage to medieval models. The main quadrangle at the University of Sydney is another well-known example.\n\nThe originals still matter: when fire tore through Notre-Dame in 2019 people worldwide watched in grief, and its reopening in December 2024 was celebrated."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Abbot Suger want his rebuilt church at Saint-Denis to achieve?", ["A fortress-like church with thick walls and tiny windows","A church full of light, unlike dark Romanesque churches","A copy of an ancient Roman temple in the French countryside","A hall with a flat timber roof and no windows at all"], 1, "Suger saw light as a way to experience God, and his open, bright church launched Gothic architecture."],
    [1, "How did flying buttresses let builders open the walls into huge windows?", ["They replaced the stone vault with a lighter wooden roof","They made the walls much thicker so windows could be added","They carried the vault's push to piers outside the walls","They shifted the building's weight onto the new windows"], 2, "With the load handled outside, the walls between supports could become windows."],
    [2, "How did medieval glaziers create blue and red glass?", ["Colour painted onto clear glass and left to dry","Crushed gemstones mixed into the lead strips","Glass fired in a special blue kiln beside the cathedral","Metal oxides, such as cobalt, added to molten glass"], 3, "Metal oxides coloured the molten glass, and lead and iron frames held the pieces together."],
    [3, "How did medieval masons design structures without modern structural maths?", ["With geometry, experience and rules of thumb","With detailed computer models testing every load","By copying Roman engineering manuals exactly","By following written engineering formulas"], 0, "Trial and error, including failures like Beauvais in 1284, taught builders what worked."],
    [4, "What explains why Australia has many Gothic-style buildings?", ["Medieval masons migrated to Australia in the twelfth century","The Gothic Revival spread with the British Empire in the 1800s","Gothic was the only style Australian builders knew how to use","Cathedrals were moved stone by stone from Europe"], 1, "British architects saw Gothic as a Christian national style, and it travelled to places like Sydney and Melbourne."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Medieval masons raised stone ceilings with no equations. Here is how they did it."});

WB.L("film-editing-invisible-art", "arts", "beginner", "Film editing: the invisible art",
  "The best editing is the kind you never notice. Yet every cut in a film is a decision that shapes what you feel, what you understand and even what you think an actor is doing.",
  [
    ["A face that changed meaning", "The meaning of a shot depends heavily on what comes before and after it, which gives the editor great power.",
      "Around the time of the 1917 revolution in Russia, the young filmmaker Lev Kuleshov tried an experiment. According to the usual account, he cut a single shot of an actor's neutral face with different images: a bowl of soup, a child in a coffin, a woman on a sofa. Audiences reportedly praised the subtle performance, seeing hunger, grief or desire, though the face was identical each time.\n\nThe original footage is lost, and modern attempts to repeat the test have given mixed results, so the story is probably tidier than the reality. Still, filmmakers widely accept the core idea: a shot's meaning depends heavily on what comes before and after it. That is the editor's power."],
    ["Scissors and glue", "For most of film history, editing meant physically cutting and joining film, and its language still survives in digital tools.",
      "For most of the twentieth century, editors worked with their hands. They watched footage on machines such as the upright Moviola or the flatbed Steenbeck, marked the frames they wanted, then cut the film with a splicer and joined it with tape or cement. Unused shots hung in strips from bins. Every change meant physical cutting, so trying alternatives took skill and patience.\n\nFrom the late 1980s and 1990s, digital non-linear systems such as Avid, and later Final Cut and Premiere, changed everything. Footage could be rearranged instantly and every version saved. Editors still say 'cuts', 'trims' and 'bins', words from the days of scissors, and many say the craft itself, deciding what to show and when, has not changed."],
    ["Rules that hide the joins", "Continuity editing uses conventions such as the 180-degree rule and cutting on action so viewers follow the story without noticing cuts.",
      "Hollywood developed continuity editing so viewers follow the story without noticing the cuts. The 180-degree rule says to imagine a line between two people in conversation and keep the camera on one side, so each character consistently faces the same direction on screen. The shot and reverse shot cuts back and forth between speakers.\n\nEditors also cut on action. If a character starts to sit in a wide shot, the cut to a closer shot happens mid-movement, and the eye follows the motion across the join. An eyeline match shows someone looking off screen, then cuts to what they see. Together these tricks build a sense of continuous space that may have been filmed over several days."],
    ["Collision and broken rules", "Montage and deliberate rule-breaking show that cuts can create new ideas, and breaking a rule only works because the audience knows it.",
      "In the 1920s Sergei Eisenstein believed that cutting two contrasting shots together could produce a third idea in neither. In Battleship Potemkin (1925), the Odessa Steps sequence builds terror through rapid cuts between boots, faces and a runaway pram, stretching a moment beyond real time. In Kubrick's 2001: A Space Odyssey, a bone thrown into the air cuts to a spacecraft, spanning millions of years.\n\nOnce audiences learned the rules, filmmakers broke them. Godard's Breathless (1960) used jump cuts, abrupt jumps forward within one shot, which felt raw and helped define the French New Wave. Jump cuts are now everywhere, including online videos. Smash cuts jump from quiet to shock, and crosscutting alternates between simultaneous events to build suspense."],
    ["Rhythm, emotion and Fury Road", "Great editors put emotion first and guide the viewer's eye, which is why the best editing goes unnoticed.",
      "Editor Walter Murch, in his book In the Blink of an Eye, suggested a cut feels natural because it works a bit like a blink, which tends to happen as we shift thoughts. He ranked emotion first in a good cut, above story, rhythm and even perfect continuity: if a cut feels right, a small mismatch in a glass of water will not matter. Shots have also become shorter over the decades.\n\nMargaret Sixel, born in South Africa and building her career in Australia, edited George Miller's Mad Max: Fury Road (2015) and won the Academy Award for Best Film Editing. She kept the key action in the centre of the frame from shot to shot, so even quick cuts stay readable. Try counting the cuts in a scene and see how many slip by."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the main lesson of the Kuleshov effect?", ["An actor's face has no effect on how audiences feel","Audiences always see the same emotion in the same face","A shot's meaning depends heavily on the shots around it","Neutral expressions are the hardest to edit"], 2, "The same face seemed to show hunger, grief or desire depending on what it was cut with."],
    [1, "How did digital editing change the craft compared with cutting film by hand?", ["Footage could be rearranged instantly and versions saved","Editors no longer had to choose which shots to show","Films could no longer follow the 180-degree rule","Cuts became impossible to hide from the audience"], 0, "Digital tools made trying alternatives fast, though many editors say the craft of choosing what to show and when has not changed."],
    [2, "Why do editors often cut on action?", ["It makes scenes cheaper to film on set","It lets actors skip the difficult movements","It breaks the 180-degree rule on purpose","Motion draws the eye across, hiding the join"], 3, "Cutting mid-movement keeps the viewer's attention on the action, so the cut goes unnoticed."],
    [3, "Why does breaking an editing rule work as a technique?", ["Audiences cannot tell any editing rules exist","Audiences know the rules, so a break stands out","The rules only apply to silent films","Rules have no real effect on how stories feel"], 1, "Jump cuts felt raw in 1960 because audiences were used to smooth continuity."],
    [4, "What did Walter Murch rank first in judging a good cut?", ["Perfect continuity between every pair of shots","The speed and pace of the cuts across a film","The emotion that the cut makes the viewer feel","Clear plot progress at every moment"], 2, "If a cut makes you feel the right thing, a small continuity slip will not matter."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Every cut steers what you feel. Why the best editing is the kind you never notice."});

WB.L("story-of-pigments", "arts", "beginner", "The colourful history of paint",
  "Some colours were once worth more than gold. Others were made from snails, insects or even Egyptian mummies. The story of pigments is a story of trade, chemistry, danger and luck.",
  [
    ["Earth in your palm", "Ochre, a clay coloured by iron oxides, is one of humanity's oldest pigments and is still used today.",
      "Pick up a lump of red or yellow earth, grind it to powder, mix it with water or fat, and you have paint. Ochre, a clay coloured by iron oxides, is among the oldest pigments people used. Humans were collecting and processing it tens of thousands of years ago, and it appears in ancient rock art worldwide.\n\nIn Australia, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have used ochre for a very long time, for painting rock, bark and bodies, and in ceremony. Some sources were highly valued and traded over long distances. Heating yellow ochre turns it red, a trick early people seem to have discovered. Earth colours such as raw sienna and burnt umber remain on every artist's palette today."],
    ["Colours worth more than gold", "Ultramarine from lapis lazuli and Tyrian purple from sea snails were so costly and laborious that they signalled status and power.",
      "The finest medieval and Renaissance blue came from the mines of Badakhshan in what is now Afghanistan, where lapis lazuli was dug from the mountains. Grinding the stone gave a dull greyish powder, so craftspeople kneaded it in a dough of wax and resin and washed out the pure blue particles. The result, ultramarine, meaning 'from beyond the sea', could cost more than gold by weight. Contracts sometimes specified how much a painter had to use, and it was often kept for the robes of the Virgin Mary.\n\nThe city of Tyre, in today's Lebanon, became famous for a dye from murex sea snails. Each snail yields a tiny amount, so the dye needed vast numbers and smelled awful. In ancient Rome, a purple stripe marked senators, and emperors claimed the deepest purple for themselves."],
    ["Beautiful and deadly", "Many of history's loveliest pigments, including lead white and arsenic greens, were poisonous.",
      "Many old pigments were dangerous. Lead white, the main white paint for centuries, was toxic, and painters who handled it often risked lead poisoning. In 1775 the Swedish chemist Carl Wilhelm Scheele made a vivid green from copper and arsenic. Scheele's green, and the related emerald green, became fashionable in the nineteenth century for wallpapers, clothing and even sweets. They released arsenic as they aged or got damp, harming the people who lived with them.\n\nThen there was mummy brown, a rich paint made from ground-up Egyptian mummies and sold by colourmakers until the twentieth century. Some artists were horrified when they learned what it contained. Modern artists' paints are far safer, and labels warn about the few hazardous pigments still sold."],
    ["Accidents and the paint tube", "Accidental discoveries like Prussian blue and mauveine, plus the portable paint tube, transformed painting in the 1700s and 1800s.",
      "Around 1706 in Berlin, a colourmaker trying to make red ended up with deep blue, apparently because an ingredient was contaminated. Prussian blue, cheaper than ultramarine, became one of the first modern synthetic pigments, used in painting, printing and engineers' blueprints.\n\nIn 1856 eighteen-year-old William Henry Perkin, a London chemistry student, tried to make the malaria drug quinine from coal tar. He failed, but noticed a purple residue that dyed silk beautifully. He called it mauveine and started a business. Mauve became a craze and helped launch the synthetic dye industry.\n\nIn 1841 John Goffe Rand, an American portrait painter in London, patented a collapsible metal paint tube with a screw cap. Paint became portable, making it easier to paint outdoors, as the French Impressionists often did."],
    ["Colours still being invented", "New pigments such as YInMn blue and Vantablack keep appearing, and some spark arguments over who may use them.",
      "The search for new colours has not ended. In 2009 a team led by chemist Mas Subramanian at Oregon State University was experimenting with materials for electronics when a sample came out of the furnace a brilliant blue. Named YInMn blue after yttrium, indium and manganese, it was the first new inorganic blue pigment in about two centuries, and it is very stable.\n\nIn 2014 a British company announced Vantablack, a coating of tiny carbon tubes that absorbs almost all visible light, making objects look like holes in space. When the sculptor Anish Kapoor obtained exclusive rights to use it in art, other artists protested. The artist Stuart Semple responded by releasing his own pink paint that anyone could buy except Kapoor."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How can ochre be turned into paint?", ["By heating it in a furnace until it melts","By grinding it and mixing it with water or fat","By soaking it in vinegar and straining it","By kneading it in wax and resin for weeks"], 1, "Ochre needs only grinding and a binder, which is why people have used it for tens of thousands of years."],
    [1, "Why was ultramarine so expensive?", ["It was made from huge numbers of sea snails that had to be collected","Painters were banned by law from using it on ordinary works","Its stone came from distant mines and needed long processing","It was produced only in tiny amounts by accident in laboratories"], 2, "Lapis lazuli from distant Badakhshan needed a long extraction process, so it could cost more than gold."],
    [2, "Why were Scheele's green and emerald green dangerous?", ["They contained lead that built up in painters' bodies","They were made from ground-up Egyptian mummies","They faded quickly and caused skin burns","They released arsenic as they aged or got damp"], 3, "These fashionable greens harmed people who lived with them by releasing arsenic."],
    [3, "How did the collapsible paint tube change painting?", ["It made paint portable, so artists could paint outdoors","It made paint cheaper by using new synthetic dyes","It let artists mix any colour without needing pigments","It swapped oil paint for water-based ink"], 0, "Portable paint helped artists such as the Impressionists capture changing outdoor light."],
    [4, "What was notable about YInMn blue's discovery?", ["Ground from a rare blue stone mined in Afghanistan","It appeared by accident during electronics research","Created deliberately to imitate the colour of ancient Tyrian purple","Designed from the start to absorb almost all visible light"], 1, "Found by accident while testing electronic materials, it was the first new inorganic blue in about two centuries. Chemistry can still produce new colours by chance."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Some colours were once worth more than gold, and one was made from mummies."});

WB.L("ukiyo-e-great-wave", "arts", "intermediate", "The Great Wave and the floating world",
  "A giant wave curls like a claw over three tiny boats while Mount Fuji sits calmly in the distance. This Japanese print is one of the most famous images on Earth. Here is the world that made it.",
  [
    ["Pictures of the floating world", "Ukiyo-e were affordable woodblock prints celebrating the urban pleasures of Edo-period Japan.",
      "During the Edo period, from 1603 to 1868, Japan was largely closed to the outside world and ruled from Edo, today's Tokyo. As cities grew, prosperous merchants and artisans built a lively popular culture of theatre, teahouses, fashion and pleasure districts. The word 'ukiyo' once meant a sorrowful, fleeting world in a Buddhist sense, but came to mean the 'floating world' of urban entertainment, enjoyed for the moment.\n\nUkiyo-e, pictures of the floating world, showed that culture. Early ones were paintings, but woodblock prints made them affordable to ordinary townspeople. Popular subjects included kabuki actors, celebrated beauties and courtesans, legendary heroes and, later, landscapes and travel scenes. They were the posters and celebrity photos of their day."],
    ["A team effort", "A ukiyo-e print was made by a publisher, designer, carver and printer working together, using many blocks.",
      "We usually name a single artist, but a ukiyo-e print was a team product. The publisher came up with the project, paid for it and sold the results. The designer, the artist we remember, drew the image in ink on thin paper. A skilled carver pasted that drawing face down onto a block of cherry wood and carved away everything except the lines, destroying the original in the process. Extra blocks were carved for each colour.\n\nA printer then brushed water-based ink onto each block in turn and pressed damp paper onto it by hand with a flat pad called a baren. Small notches called kento made sure each colour lined up precisely. A single print could need a dozen or more blocks."],
    ["Brocade pictures", "From 1765 reliable registration allowed full-colour prints, launching a golden age of ukiyo-e.",
      "Early woodblock prints were black and white or had a few colours added by hand. In 1765 the artist Suzuki Harunobu and his collaborators produced prints using many separate colour blocks, made possible by the reliable registration system. These full-colour prints were called nishiki-e, or 'brocade pictures', because their rich colours reminded people of luxurious woven silk.\n\nThey kicked off a golden age. Kitagawa Utamaro became famous for elegant portraits of women, often close-up half-length figures. Toshusai Sharaku produced striking, almost caricature-like portraits of kabuki actors in a career of less than a year, before vanishing from the record. Prints were produced in large editions and sold in shops, many relatively cheaply, part of the everyday visual life of the city."],
    ["Hokusai and Hiroshige", "Hokusai's Great Wave, made around 1831, and Hiroshige's atmospheric travel series turned landscape into a popular print subject.",
      "Katsushika Hokusai, born in 1760, worked for roughly seven decades and changed his name around thirty times. Around 1831 he designed Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji. Its most famous image, Under the Wave off Kanagawa, now called The Great Wave, shows boats dwarfed by a towering wave whose foam breaks into claw-like fingers, with Fuji small in the distance. It uses Prussian blue, an imported synthetic pigment cheaper and more lasting than earlier blues. Thousands of copies were probably printed.\n\nHis younger rival Utagawa Hiroshige became famous for gentler, atmospheric landscapes. His 1830s series The Fifty-three Stations of the Tokaido followed the road from Edo to Kyoto through rain, snow and dusk, part souvenir and part journey for those who could not travel. Soft colour gradations, made by wiping the ink on the block, put viewers in the scene."],
    ["Japan meets Europe", "Japanese prints inspired a European craze called Japonisme, shaping Van Gogh, Monet, Degas and Cassatt.",
      "In the 1850s Japan was forced to open its ports to foreign trade, and Japanese goods flowed into Europe. A popular story says a French artist found Hokusai's sketches used as packing around porcelain; it may be partly legend, but prints certainly arrived in large numbers. Japonisme swept through Western art and design. Artists admired bold flat colour, strong outlines, unusual high or low viewpoints and off-centre compositions, which differed from Europe's realistic perspective.\n\nVan Gogh and his brother Theo collected hundreds of prints in Paris, and Vincent painted copies of two Hiroshige prints with his own intense colours. Monet's collection still hangs at Giverny, and his Japanese-style footbridge appears in his garden paintings. Degas and Cassatt borrowed the cropped, off-centre compositions. The floating world helped shape modern Western art."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What made ukiyo-e affordable to ordinary townspeople?", ["Artists painted each one by hand very quickly","The government paid for every print","Many copies could be printed from the same blocks","They were printed on cheap imported paper"], 2, "Printing in large editions turned images of the floating world into everyday goods."],
    [1, "Why was a print a team product rather than one person's work?", ["The designer carved and printed every block alone","The publisher drew the image and sold the paintings","Each colour was painted by a separate artist","Four specialists each handled a different stage"], 3, "The artist we remember was only one of four people whose skills made a print."],
    [2, "What allowed full-colour nishiki-e prints from the 1760s?", ["A registration system that kept colour blocks aligned","The import of European printing presses","Hand-painting every print after it was printed","Switching from woodblocks to copper plates"], 0, "Kento notches let printers align a dozen or more blocks precisely."],
    [3, "How did Hiroshige's prints differ from Hokusai's Great Wave?", ["Using only black ink with no colour at all in the final prints","Gentler and more atmospheric, with weather and soft colour fades","Showing mainly kabuki actors in dramatic close-up portraits","Printed in only a few copies for wealthy private collectors"], 1, "Hiroshige's rain, snow and soft gradations of colour made viewers feel they stood in the scene."],
    [4, "What did European artists admire in Japanese prints?", ["Realistic perspective drawn carefully from life in the studio","Heavy shading and dark backgrounds in the Renaissance manner","Bold flat colour, strong outlines and unusual viewpoints","Detailed portraits of European royalty and their courts"], 2, "These ideas differed from the Renaissance tradition of realistic perspective, and shaped modern Western art."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A tiny Mount Fuji sits behind the world's most famous wave. Meet the team that made it."});

WB.L("art-forgery", "arts", "intermediate", "Art forgery: the con artists who fooled the experts",
  "One forger sold a fake Vermeer to a Nazi leader. Another was caught by a single tube of white paint. The history of art forgery is full of daring tricks, and it raises a surprisingly hard question: why does a fake matter?",
  [
    ["The forger who fooled Goring", "Han van Meegeren invented new 'Vermeers' and used baked synthetic resin to fake the hardness of age.",
      "In May 1945 Dutch police arrested painter and dealer Han van Meegeren after a 'Vermeer' was traced from the collection of the senior Nazi Hermann Goring. His defence was astonishing: it was not a national treasure, because he had painted it himself. Disbelieving investigators had him paint a new 'Vermeer' under supervision. In 1947 he was convicted of forgery and fraud, not collaboration, and sentenced to a year in prison, but died before serving it. To some Dutch people he was a folk hero.\n\nHe invented religious scenes, as some experts thought Vermeer had an early religious phase. He reused genuine seventeenth century canvases and mixed pigments with phenol-formaldehyde resin, the material of Bakelite, then baked the painting hard. Rolling the canvas created cracks, and he rubbed ink into them. In 1937 an expert hailed his Supper at Emmaus."],
    ["Inventing a collection", "Wolfgang Beltracchi faked 'lost' works and their paperwork, and was caught by an anachronistic pigment.",
      "German forger Wolfgang Beltracchi may have been even more ambitious. Instead of copying known paintings, he created works that might plausibly have existed, often filling gaps where a real artist's painting was recorded in a catalogue but lost. Just as important was the paperwork. He and his wife invented a story that the pictures came from her grandfather's collection, and faked an old photograph of her posing as her own grandmother in front of several forgeries.\n\nFor years the works passed through auction houses and dealers, sometimes selling for millions. His undoing was science. One painting supposedly from 1914 contained titanium white, a pigment not yet available to artists then. In 2011 he was sentenced to six years in prison."],
    ["Science and the trained eye", "Laboratories test whether materials could really be old, while connoisseurs and provenance can be fooled by hopeful stories.",
      "Today suspicious paintings are examined like crime scenes. Many pigments have known invention dates, so an impossible colour is a giveaway. X-rays reveal hidden layers, infrared shows the underdrawing, and tree-ring dating estimates when panel wood was cut. Nuclear tests in the 1950s and 1960s spiked carbon-14 in the atmosphere, so plants grown afterwards carry a modern signature that old materials do not.\n\nBefore laboratories, authenticity rested on connoisseurs, who knew an artist's hand like a friend's handwriting. That still matters, but experts can be swayed by what they hope to find, and by a convincing provenance, the history of ownership. A large scandal at New York's Knoedler gallery, which closed in 2011, showed that convincing paperwork and confident dealers could carry fakes into major collections."],
    ["Fakes closer to home", "Australia has had disputes over authenticity, and imitation Aboriginal art harms artists, so buy through community art centres or reputable dealers.",
      "Australia has not escaped. A Brett Whiteley fraud case saw a conviction in 2016 overturned on appeal the following year, showing how hard forgery is to prove in court.\n\nAboriginal art faces its own problems. Because it is popular with tourists and collectors, cheap imitations with no connection to the communities and cultures they copy have been widespread. This harms artists and misrepresents their culture. Many Aboriginal art centres now supply certificates and keep careful records of who made each work.\n\nIf you are buying, it helps to look for works sold through community art centres or reputable dealers. The Indigenous Art Code is a voluntary industry code worth checking."],
    ["Why does a fake matter?", "Forgery makes us ask what we value in art: the achievement, the connection to the maker, or only how it looks.",
      "Here is the puzzle. When experts believed Van Meegeren's Supper at Emmaus was a Vermeer, they found it profound. Once exposed, the same canvas seemed clumsy to many viewers, and its value fell dramatically. The paint did not change, so what did?\n\nOne answer is that we value art partly as an achievement: a genuine Vermeer is a particular person solving particular problems in seventeenth century Delft, and a forgery fakes that history. Another is that an artwork carries a connection to its maker, like a letter in someone's own handwriting.\n\nFraud also causes real harm to buyers and to an artist's reputation, since fakes can distort what we think an artist was like. Forgery makes us ask what we really value in art."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Van Meegeren mix his paint with synthetic resin and bake the painting?", ["To make the colours brighter than old paintings","To keep the canvas from cracking in storage","To make the painting waterproof for shipping","To harden fresh paint so it seemed centuries old"], 3, "Baking the resin hardened the paint so it behaved like centuries-old work."],
    [1, "What exposed Beltracchi's forgery of a painting supposedly from 1914?", ["Titanium white, a pigment not yet available","A witness who saw him painting it","A misspelt signature on the canvas","An inventory error in the auction catalogue"], 0, "Titanium white was not yet available to artists in 1914, a scientific giveaway that no story could explain."],
    [2, "Why is provenance a target for forgers?", ["A convincing history can make a fake seem genuine","Laboratories can test nothing except provenance","Auction houses never check ownership records","It lets forgers avoid period materials altogether"], 0, "A plausible history of ownership can make experts see what they hope to find."],
    [3, "Which step helps when buying Aboriginal art?", ["Choosing whichever work is cheapest at a market","Buying from any tourist stall you pass","Buying via community art centres or reputable dealers","Avoiding certificates because they are unreliable"], 2, "Art centres keep careful records of who made each work, protecting both buyers and artists."],
    [4, "What does the Van Meegeren story suggest about how we value art?", ["Only the physical paint and canvas matter","Forgeries are always easy for experts to spot","A work's value stays the same once exposed as fake","We value achievement, not only appearance"], 3, "The same canvas lost value once it was no longer a genuine Vermeer, though the paint was unchanged."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"One forger was caught by a single pigment that did not yet exist. Which one?"});

WB.L("opera-explained", "arts", "beginner", "Opera, explained: drama at full volume",
  "Opera combines music, theatre, poetry, costume and spectacle, and its singers fill huge halls without microphones. Here is how the most extravagant art form began and how to enjoy it.",
  [
    ["Reviving the Greeks", "Opera began in Italy around 1600 in an attempt to recreate sung Greek drama, and public opera houses made it popular.",
      "In the late 1500s scholars, poets and musicians in Florence, the Florentine Camerata, became fascinated by Greek tragedy. They believed, partly correctly, that Greek plays were sung or chanted, and tried setting drama to music with a single voice singing clearly over simple accompaniment. This led to the first operas around 1600. The earliest still regularly staged is Monteverdi's L'Orfeo, first performed in Mantua in 1607, retelling the myth of Orpheus in the underworld.\n\nEarly operas were for princes at court. In 1637 the first public opera house opened in Venice, and anyone with a ticket could attend. Audiences chatted, ate and played cards. Baroque stars included castrati, male singers castrated before puberty, a cruel practice that died out in the nineteenth century."],
    ["The words you need", "A few terms, such as libretto, recitative and aria, make opera much easier to follow.",
      "A little vocabulary helps enormously. The libretto, meaning 'little book', is the opera's text, written by a librettist, while the composer writes the music. Many operas begin with an overture, an orchestral piece that sets the mood before the curtain rises.\n\nMuch of the story moves forward in recitative, singing close to the rhythms of speech, often lightly accompanied, where plot and conversation happen. Then the action pauses for an aria, a solo song in which a character expresses feelings at length, where singers show off.\n\nDuets, trios and larger ensembles let several characters sing at once, sometimes each with different words and emotions, something spoken theatre cannot easily do. The chorus represents crowds, soldiers, villagers or spirits."],
    ["The great composers", "A small group of composers, from Mozart to Puccini to Wagner, wrote the operas most often staged today.",
      "Mozart, writing in the 1780s and early 1790s, gave opera human characters: The Marriage of Figaro, Don Giovanni and The Magic Flute. In the early nineteenth century Rossini, Bellini and Donizetti perfected bel canto, 'beautiful singing', with dazzling vocal agility. Verdi (Rigoletto, La Traviata, Aida) and Puccini (La Boheme, Tosca, Madama Butterfly) combined thrilling melodies with intense drama, and remain among the most performed. They make good starting points.\n\nRichard Wagner wanted opera as a total work of art. His four-opera Ring cycle takes around fifteen hours. Short musical themes, leitmotifs, represent characters and ideas, a technique film composers borrowed. His Bayreuth theatre, opened in 1876, hid the orchestra and darkened the hall, helping create modern quiet, dark audiences. His antisemitic writings make him a troubling figure."],
    ["Filling a hall unamplified", "Trained opera voices cut through an orchestra with breath control and a concentration of energy around three thousand cycles per second.",
      "An opera singer may need to be heard over dozens of players in a theatre seating a couple of thousand people, without a microphone. Part of the answer is training: years developing breath control and an efficient, open throat.\n\nAnother part is acoustics. Researchers have found that trained classical singers, particularly men, often produce a concentration of energy at high frequencies, around three thousand cycles per second, sometimes called the singer's formant. An orchestra is comparatively quiet in that range, and human ears are especially sensitive to it, so the voice carries over the instruments.\n\nVoices are classified by range, high to low: soprano, mezzo-soprano and contralto for women, and tenor, baritone and bass for men. Composers write roles for particular voice types."],
    ["Opera in Australia", "Australia produced the sopranos Nellie Melba and Joan Sutherland, and surtitles now make opera easy to follow.",
      "Australia has a proud opera tradition. Helen Porter Mitchell, born in Melbourne in 1861, took the stage name Nellie Melba in honour of her home city and became one of the world's most celebrated sopranos. Peach Melba and Melba toast were named after her.\n\nJoan Sutherland, born in Sydney in 1926, became a superstar of bel canto opera, nicknamed La Stupenda. The main opera theatre at the Sydney Opera House bears her name.\n\nToday Opera Australia is the national company, and state companies perform around the country. If opera seems intimidating, remember that most performances show translated text as surtitles above the stage, a practice that spread from the 1980s, so you can follow every word."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the Florentine Camerata create the first operas?", ["They wanted to replace church music with theatre","They were hired to entertain the public in Venice","They were trying to invent a cheaper type of theatre","They wanted to recreate ancient Greek drama as sung"], 3, "Setting drama to music with a clear single voice led to opera around 1600."],
    [1, "What happens in an aria?", ["A character sings at length about their feelings","The plot moves quickly in speech-like singing","The orchestra plays alone before the curtain rises","The chorus sings as a crowd"], 0, "Recitative moves the plot, while arias pause it so a character can express emotion."],
    [2, "What are leitmotifs in Wagner's operas?", ["Spoken introductions given before the start of each act","Short musical themes that represent characters, objects or ideas","Large choruses that represent crowds, soldiers or spirits","The printed list of characters in the libretto"], 1, "Leitmotifs let the orchestra comment on the drama, a technique film composers later borrowed."],
    [3, "Why can an opera singer be heard over an orchestra?", ["Hidden microphones built into the theatre walls and stage floor","Singing louder than every instrument at all pitches at once","Training, plus energy at high frequencies where the orchestra is quieter","The orchestra is told to play softly whenever the singer enters"], 2, "The singer's formant sits where orchestras are weaker and ears are most sensitive."],
    [4, "How can newcomers follow opera sung in another language?", ["The orchestra plays a summary first","Singers pause to speak a translation","The audience must learn the language","Translated text appears above the stage"], 3, "Surtitles, common since the 1980s, make every word easy to follow."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Singers fill huge halls without microphones. The secret is partly in the physics of sound."});

WB.L("tuning-and-temperament", "arts", "advanced", "Why every piano is slightly out of tune",
  "Ancient thinkers noticed that beautiful harmonies follow simple number ratios. Then musicians discovered those ratios don't quite fit together. The compromise they reached shapes every song you hear.",
  [
    ["Harmony is numbers", "Pleasing musical intervals correspond to simple ratios, which you can test by pressing a guitar string.",
      "Legend credits Pythagoras with noticing that musical harmony follows numbers, though the story of him hearing blacksmiths' hammers is almost certainly myth. The basic discovery is real, and you can test it on a guitar. Pluck an open string, then press it at the halfway point and pluck again: the note is an octave higher, because the vibrating length has halved and the frequency has doubled, a ratio of 2 to 1.\n\nPress a third of the way along, so two thirds vibrates, and you get a perfect fifth, a ratio of 3 to 2, the interval at the start of many familiar tunes. A ratio of 4 to 3 gives a perfect fourth, and 5 to 4 a major third. Simple ratios sound smooth and stable together."],
    ["Why simple ratios sound sweet", "Notes in simple ratios share overtones and produce little beating, so they sound calm and clear.",
      "A string or voice sounds a note at its main frequency and also at whole-number multiples of it, faint higher tones called overtones that give each instrument its colour. If two notes are in a simple ratio, many of their overtones line up exactly.\n\nWhen two tones are close but not quite matched, they interfere and produce a regular wobble in loudness called beating. Play two notes that differ by two cycles per second and you hear the sound pulse twice a second. Piano tuners listen for exactly these beats, slowing or speeding them to set each interval. Simple ratios produce little beating, so they sound calm and clear."],
    ["The unavoidable comma", "Stacking twelve pure fifths overshoots seven octaves by the Pythagorean comma, so no keyboard can have every interval pure.",
      "Here is the snag. Start on C and go up by a pure fifth to G, then D, then A, and keep going. After twelve fifths you have passed through every note and should land on a C, seven octaves above where you began. But the numbers disagree. Twelve pure fifths multiply out to about 129.7 times the starting frequency, while seven octaves give exactly 128.\n\nThe gap is the Pythagorean comma. Musicians measure intervals in cents, with 100 cents in a modern semitone and 1200 in an octave, and the comma is about 23.5 cents, roughly a quarter of a semitone. It is a mathematical fact: no keyboard can have all its fifths and octaves perfectly pure at once."],
    ["Wolves, meantone and Bach", "Earlier tunings made some keys sweet and others unusable, and well temperaments made every key playable with its own character.",
      "Pythagorean tuning kept fifths pure, but its major thirds sounded harsh. Renaissance musicians adopted meantone temperaments, narrowing the fifths so the common major thirds came out pure or nearly so. The leftover error piled up in one fifth that howled, called the wolf fifth. Nearby keys sounded lovely, but distant keys were sour or unusable, and changing key could mean retuning.\n\nBy the late seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries, musicians wanted every key. Well temperaments spread the comma unevenly, so no interval howled, though common keys stayed sweeter and remote keys more tense, giving each key its own flavour. In 1722 Bach completed the first book of The Well-Tempered Clavier, twenty-four preludes and fugues, one in every major and minor key. Scholars still debate his tuning, but it was probably not modern equal temperament."],
    ["Equal temperament and beyond", "Modern pianos split the octave into twelve equal steps, trading pure intervals for freedom, while singers and other cultures make different choices.",
      "Equal temperament divides the octave into twelve exactly equal semitones, each a frequency ratio of the twelfth root of two, about 1.0595. Zhu Zaiyu in China worked it out in the late 1500s, as did Simon Stevin in Europe. It became the piano standard in the nineteenth century, and every key now sounds the same. The price is that almost every interval is slightly impure. Fifths are about two cents narrow, but major thirds are about fourteen cents wider than the pure 5 to 4 ratio, a shimmer our ears accept as normal.\n\nChoirs and string quartets tune thirds by ear so harmony rings. Tuners stretch piano octaves a little wider because stiff strings run sharp. Concert pitch sets the A above middle C at 440 cycles per second. Gamelan and Arabic music use scales that fall between piano keys."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What happens when you press a guitar string exactly halfway along?", ["The note drops an octave in pitch","The note becomes a perfect fifth higher","The note rises an octave in pitch","The note stays the same but quieter"], 2, "Halving the vibrating length doubles the frequency, a 2 to 1 ratio."],
    [1, "Why do notes in simple ratios sound smooth together?", ["They are always played at exactly the same volume","They are made without any overtones at all","They always come from the same single string","Their overtones line up, so there is little beating"], 3, "Matching overtones and few wobbles give a calm, clear sound."],
    [2, "Why can't a keyboard have every fifth and octave perfectly pure?", ["Pure fifths are too quiet to hear on a piano","Octaves are too wide to fit on one keyboard","The comma is only an old measuring error","Twelve pure fifths overshoot seven octaves slightly"], 3, "The numbers disagree: twelve pure fifths are about 129.7 times the starting frequency, but seven octaves are exactly 128."],
    [3, "What was the advantage of well temperaments over meantone?", ["Every interval became perfectly pure in all keys","Every key became playable, each with its own flavour","They removed the need to tune keyboards at all","They made every distant key sound just like C"], 1, "No interval howled, so composers could write in any key, each with its own flavour."],
    [4, "What is the trade-off of equal temperament?", ["Every interval is pure, but few keys are usable","Only one key can be played on a given piano","Any key works, but most intervals are slightly impure","Pianos must be retuned whenever the key changes"], 2, "Freedom to change key comes at the cost of slightly impure intervals, such as wide major thirds."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Every piano is slightly out of tune, on purpose. Here is the maths behind it."});

